---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../learning/styles/learning.css';
import '../../lessons/higgs-hunt/higgs-hunt.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Higgs Hunt — ROOT Quest"
  description="Build a diphoton selection visually, discover a mass structure, and recognize the equivalent ROOT analysis."
>
  <header class="hunt-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">ROOT Quest · Main experience</p>
    <h1>Higgs Hunt</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Begin with one collision. End with the ROOT analysis you already understand.
    </p>
  </header>

  <article class="hunt-shell" aria-labelledby="hunt-stage-title">
    <header class="hunt-progress">
      <span id="hunt-stage-label">1 of 5 · See</span>
      <progress id="hunt-progress" max="5" value="1" aria-label="Higgs Hunt progress">1 of 5</progress>
      <span class="sample-label">Fixed synthetic sample</span>
    </header>

    <div class="hunt-stage-viewport">
      <section class="hunt-stage" data-hunt-stage="0" aria-labelledby="hunt-stage-title">
        <header class="stage-copy">
          <p class="stage-kicker">One collision</p>
          <h2 id="hunt-stage-title" tabindex="-1">Find the two photons</h2>
          <p>
            Select the reconstructed objects that could form a diphoton candidate.
            You can always change your choice.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="event-workspace">
          <figure class="event-display">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 520 400" role="img" aria-labelledby="event-title event-description">
              <title id="event-title">Conceptual transverse view of one collision event</title>
              <desc id="event-description">Two photon-like energy deposits and one broad jet emerge from the collision point.</desc>
              <circle class="detector-ring detector-ring--outer" cx="260" cy="200" r="166"></circle>
              <circle class="detector-ring" cx="260" cy="200" r="118"></circle>
              <circle class="detector-ring detector-ring--inner" cx="260" cy="200" r="55"></circle>
              <line class="detector-axis" x1="74" y1="200" x2="446" y2="200"></line>
              <line class="detector-axis" x1="260" y1="14" x2="260" y2="386"></line>

              <g class="event-object event-object--photon" data-object-visual="photon-1">
                <line x1="260" y1="200" x2="402" y2="117"></line>
                <rect x="394" y="103" width="28" height="28" rx="6"></rect>
                <text x="430" y="102">A</text>
              </g>
              <g class="event-object event-object--photon" data-object-visual="photon-2">
                <line x1="260" y1="200" x2="116" y2="292"></line>
                <rect x="97" y="282" width="28" height="28" rx="6"></rect>
                <text x="84" y="328">B</text>
              </g>
              <g class="event-object event-object--jet" data-object-visual="jet-1">
                <path d="M260 200 L309 42 L364 72 Z"></path>
                <circle cx="325" cy="65" r="16"></circle>
                <text x="370" y="67">C</text>
              </g>
              <circle class="collision-point" cx="260" cy="200" r="8"></circle>
            </svg>
            <figcaption>
              Simplified teaching view—not a detector display. Photons end as compact
              calorimeter deposits; the jet is broader.
            </figcaption>
          </figure>

          <div class="object-panel">
            <h3>Choose a pair</h3>
            <div class="object-buttons" aria-label="Reconstructed objects">
              <button type="button" data-object-id="photon-1" aria-pressed="false">
                <span><strong>Object A</strong><small>48 GeV · compact deposit</small></span>
              </button>
              <button type="button" data-object-id="photon-2" aria-pressed="false">
                <span><strong>Object B</strong><small>42 GeV · compact deposit</small></span>
              </button>
              <button type="button" data-object-id="jet-1" aria-pressed="false">
                <span><strong>Object C</strong><small>36 GeV · broad spray</small></span>
              </button>
            </div>
            <rq-feedback id="object-feedback">
              <p>Select two objects and compare their detector signatures.</p>
            </rq-feedback>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="hunt-stage" data-hunt-stage="1" aria-labelledby="rule-stage-title" hidden>
        <header class="stage-copy">
          <p class="stage-kicker">From eye to rule</p>
          <h2 id="rule-stage-title" tabindex="-1">Predict what the first filter keeps</h2>
          <p>
            Manual inspection does not scale. The rule below applies the same decision
            to every event: <code>photon_n == 2</code>.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="rule-workspace">
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="rule-table">
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Event</th><th scope="col">Photons</th><th scope="col">Leading pT</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">A</th><td>2</td><td>48 GeV</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">B</th><td>1</td><td>60 GeV</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">C</th><td>2</td><td>34 GeV</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">D</th><td>3</td><td>55 GeV</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <rq-predict id="rule-prediction" answer-name="prediction">
            <form>
              <fieldset>
                <legend>Which events survive this rule?</legend>
                <div class="rq-options">
                  <label><input type="radio" name="prediction" value="a-c" required /> A and C</label>
                  <label><input type="radio" name="prediction" value="a-d" /> A and D</label>
                  <label><input type="radio" name="prediction" value="b-d" /> B and D</label>
                </div>
              </fieldset>
              <button class="rq-button rq-button--primary" type="submit">Commit prediction</button>
            </form>
            <rq-feedback data-prediction-feedback hidden></rq-feedback>
          </rq-predict>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="hunt-stage" data-hunt-stage="2" aria-labelledby="cut-stage-title" hidden>
        <header class="stage-copy">
          <p class="stage-kicker">One rule, many events</p>
          <h2 id="cut-stage-title" tabindex="-1">Move the cut and watch the trade-off</h2>
          <p>
            Tightening a transverse-momentum cut removes background—but it can also
            discard useful signal.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="cut-workspace">
          <div class="cut-visual">
            <svg id="cut-svg" viewBox="0 0 720 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="cut-title cut-description">
              <title id="cut-title">Leading photon transverse-momentum distribution</title>
              <desc id="cut-description">Events are shown along a transverse-momentum axis with a movable threshold.</desc>
              <line class="plot-axis" x1="46" y1="184" x2="688" y2="184"></line>
              <g data-cut-events></g>
              <g class="cut-marker" data-cut-marker>
                <line y1="30" y2="184"></line>
                <text y="22" text-anchor="middle"></text>
              </g>
              <text class="axis-label" x="367" y="220" text-anchor="middle">leading photon pT [GeV]</text>
            </svg>

            <label class="range-label" for="photon-pt-cut">
              Minimum leading photon pT
              <output id="photon-pt-value" for="photon-pt-cut">35 GeV</output>
            </label>
            <input id="photon-pt-cut" type="range" min="25" max="60" step="1" value="35" />
          </div>

          <div class="metric-pair" aria-label="Training metrics">
            <article class="metric metric--signal">
              <span>Signal kept</span>
              <strong id="signal-efficiency">90%</strong>
            </article>
            <article class="metric metric--background">
              <span>Background removed</span>
              <strong id="background-rejection">55%</strong>
            </article>
          </div>

          <rq-feedback id="cut-feedback" aria-live="off">
            <p>The line is the cut. Events to its left are removed from the sample.</p>
          </rq-feedback>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="hunt-stage" data-hunt-stage="3" aria-labelledby="plot-stage-title" hidden>
        <header class="stage-copy">
          <p class="stage-kicker">Events become a distribution</p>
          <h2 id="plot-stage-title" tabindex="-1">Look for structure, not individual rows</h2>
          <p>
            The labels are gone. Each selected event contributes one diphoton-mass
            value to the histogram.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="plot-workspace">
          <svg id="mass-histogram" class="mass-histogram" viewBox="0 0 720 330" role="img" aria-labelledby="mass-title mass-description">
            <title id="mass-title">Diphoton mass histogram after selection</title>
            <desc id="mass-description">A histogram of selected synthetic events between 100 and 160 GeV.</desc>
            <rect class="mass-window" x="281" y="30" width="65" height="236" rx="6"></rect>
            <line class="plot-axis" x1="52" y1="266" x2="686" y2="266"></line>
            <g data-mass-bars></g>
            <text class="axis-label" x="369" y="310" text-anchor="middle">diphoton mass [GeV]</text>
          </svg>

          <div class="plot-controls">
            <label class="range-label" for="mass-bin-count">
              Histogram bins
              <output id="mass-bin-value" for="mass-bin-count">12</output>
            </label>
            <input id="mass-bin-count" type="range" min="6" max="16" step="1" value="12" />
            <p id="mass-summary">Selected events are grouped into 12 mass intervals.</p>
          </div>

          <rq-feedback id="mass-feedback" aria-live="off">
            <p>The shaded region is near 125 GeV. Change the bins: the events stay fixed while their grouping changes.</p>
          </rq-feedback>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="hunt-stage" data-hunt-stage="4" aria-labelledby="root-stage-title" hidden>
        <header class="stage-copy stage-copy--final">
          <p class="stage-kicker">Meaning becomes syntax</p>
          <h2 id="root-stage-title" tabindex="-1">You already built this ROOT analysis</h2>
          <p>
            The code is not a new idea. It is a compact record of the selections and
            histogram you just manipulated.
          </p>
        </header>

        <div class="root-workspace">
          <div class="code-card">
            <header>
              <span>ROOT · RDataFrame</span>
              <button class="copy-button" id="copy-root-code" type="button">Copy code</button>
            </header>
            <pre><code id="root-code"></code></pre>
          </div>

          <rq-feedback id="root-feedback">
            <p><strong>Selection → distribution → code.</strong> ROOT formalizes decisions whose consequences you have already seen.</p>
          </rq-feedback>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <rq-stepper id="hunt-stepper" aria-label="Higgs Hunt stages">
      <output class="visually-hidden" data-stepper-status>Stage 1 of 5: See</output>
      <div class="hunt-navigation">
        <button type="button" data-stepper-action="previous" hidden>Back</button>
        <button class="hunt-next" type="button" data-stepper-action="next" disabled>Continue</button>
        <button type="button" data-stepper-action="reset" hidden>Start again</button>
      </div>
    </rq-stepper>
  </article>

  <p class="teaching-data-note">
    This experience uses a small fixed synthetic sample to expose cause and effect;
    it is not experimental data or a statistical claim.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  import '../../lessons/higgs-hunt/controller';
</script>
